        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Among 482 deliveries at our institute between December 2004 and May 2005, only those who delivered by cesarean section at 31-41 weeks of gestation and had UBC EPC counted and had 1-2 ml of umbilical cord plasma stored for later use at the time of delivery were selected. Pregnancies associated with premature rupture of membranes, fetal malformation, chromosome anomaly, multiple pregnancies, hypertension, renal or endocrine diseases were excluded from the study. Severe preeclampsia was defined as persistent systolic or diastolic blood pressures above 160 or 110 mm Hg, respectively, in at least two consecutive measurements, with proteinuria at least 3+ on a random urine dipstick measurement beyond 20 weeks of gestation [21] . Based on the above inclusion and exclusion criteria, 30 patients and 15 patients were eligible to be included in normal and severe preeclampsia groups, respectively, and their data were retrospectively analyzed. All patients consented to the sampling and use of their medical records in the future for research purposes, and the hospital review board approved this study.\n\nSmall for gestational age (SGA) was defined as birth weight of ! 10th percentile for corresponding gestational age. At the time of delivery, cord was clamped after fetal expulsion and subsequently UBC for EPC isolation and ELISA measurement was obtained.",
