  "content": "Topic: Prophylactic abdominal decompression in normal pregnancy \u2014 Cochrane review (search updated 2 Feb 2012)\n\nScope and objective\n- Assess effects of prophylactic antenatal abdominal decompression on admission for pre\u2010eclampsia, fetal growth, perinatal morbidity and mortality, and childhood development in healthy pregnant women.\n- Search: Cochrane Pregnancy and Childbirth Group Trials Register (2 Feb 2012). No language restrictions. No new trials identified in the update.\n\nIntervention (definition)\n- Abdominal decompression: rigid dome around the abdomen with airtight suit; intermittent negative pressure (typically \u221250 to \u2212100 mmHg) applied for 15\u201330 seconds each minute for 30 minutes, once to thrice daily, or applied with contractions during labour. Intended to improve uteroplacental blood flow.\n\nIncluded trials (3)\n1. Coxon 1973 (UK) \u2014 randomized allocation; primigravidae single fetus; decompression from ~28 weeks, 15 s/min for 30 min twice weekly. Comparison: \u221270 mmHg (n=200) versus \u221220 mmHg 'control' (n=211). Outcomes: BP, admission for pre\u2010eclampsia, birthweight, placental weight, perinatal mortality. Note: control (\u221220 mmHg) may not be an inert placebo.\n2. Liddicoat 1968 (South Africa) \u2014 randomized allocation; antenatal decompression versus routine antenatal physiotherapy classes. Offspring evaluated blind at 1, 4, 9 months and 3 years (Merrill-Palmer at 3 years). High dropout: 45% at 9 months, 56% by 3 years.\n3. Hofmeyr 1990 \u2014 review of hospital notes from the Liddicoat cohort to report perinatal data (early 1960s cohort). ~23% of results unobtainable.\n\nExcluded\n- Mathews 1968: excluded because no clinically relevant outcomes reported (20 women in labour; fetal scalp measures only).\n\nRisk of bias\n- Randomization/allocation concealment judged adequate (low risk) in included trials. Blinding variable: Coxon blinded women/attendants; offspring assessment in Liddicoat was blinded. Concern: placebo at \u221220 mmHg in Coxon may have physiological effect.\n\nMain quantitative outcomes (from pooled/individual trial data)\n- Admission for pre\u2010eclampsia: 1 study, n=411 \u2014 Risk ratio (RR) 1.06 (95% CI 0.47 to 2.38).\n- Low birthweight: 1 study, n=253 \u2014 RR 0.69 (95% CI 0.27 to 1.77).\n- Apgar score <4 at 1 minute: 1 study, n=242 \u2014 RR 1.00 (95% CI 0.21 to 4.86).\n- Stillbirth: 2 studies, n=709 \u2014 RR 4.68 (95% CI 0.80 to 27.31) (wide CI).\n- Neonatal death: 2 studies, n=705 \u2014 RR 1.11 (95% CI 0.23 to 5.43).\n- Perinatal mortality: 2 studies, n=709 \u2014 RR 2.47 (95% CI 0.77 to 7.92).\n\nOther findings\n- Mathews & Loeffler (labour study excluded from main review) found a non\u2011significant greater increase in scalp blood pH with decompression (+0.05 vs +0.01).\n- No consistent benefit found for admission for pre\u2010eclampsia, fetal growth, Apgar scores, perinatal mortality, or childhood development.\n- Small excess of perinatal deaths in decompression groups noted; may be chance (CIs wide).\n- Unexpected observations meriting further study:\n  - Liddicoat 1968: more children rated undisciplined/aggressive at 3 years in decompression group (14/89 vs 2/90).\n  - Coxon 1973: placental weights lower in high decompression group: 627 (SEM 9) g vs 653 (SEM 9) g in low decompression group.\n\nAuthors\u2019 conclusions and implications\n- Practice: Convincing evidence that antenatal abdominal decompression in uncomplicated pregnancies does not improve measured outcomes \u2014 no support for clinical prophylactic use in normal pregnancies.\n- Intrapartum abdominal decompression: insufficiently evaluated to recommend or reject.\n- Research: Future research should focus on use during labour and in complicated pregnancies; investigate psychosocial/physiological mechanisms suggested by the unexpected observations (child behaviour, placental mass).\n\nStudy/publication details\n- Review protocol first published: Issue 2, 1998; review first published: Issue 2, 1998. Review updated; 2 Feb 2012 update: new citation required but conclusions unchanged.\n- Review authors: GJ Hofmeyr (original), R Kulier (checked/modified). Internal/external supports listed (University of the Witwatersrand; Geneva Univ Hospital; South African Medical Research Council).\n\nReferences (included trials)\n- Coxon A et al., Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology (British Commonwealth) 1973;80:1081\u201385.\n- Liddicoat R., South African Medical Journal 1968;42:203\u201311.\n- Hofmeyr GJ et al., BJOG 1990;97:547\u201348.",
  "description": "Abdominal decompression was developed as a means of pain relief during labour. It has also been used for complications of pregnancy, and in healthy pregnant women in an attempt to improve fetal wellbeing and intellectual development. The objective ...",
