2026-07-26 12:06:27,064 - sciconharness.mcp_client.utils.utils - INFO - Content: [[[Evidence from three small randomized controlled trials conducted between 1967 and 1973, synthesized in a 2012 Cochrane review, suggests that antenatal abdominal decompression may improve several perinatal outcomes in women with hypertension or impaired fetal growth, including reductions in persistent pre-eclampsia (RR 0.36; 95% CI 0.18–0.72), fetal distress in labour (RR 0.37; 95% CI 0.19–0.71), low birthweight (RR 0.50; 95% CI 0.40–0.63), low one-minute Apgar scores (RR 0.26; 95% CI 0.12–0.56), and perinatal mortality (RR 0.39; 95% CI 0.22–0.71). However, the strength of this evidence is severely limited by the small number of participants (total ~356–367 women), the age of the trials, and high risks of bias from quasi-random allocation, lack of blinding, and selective reallocation of non-compliant participants. While the direction and magnitude of the observed effects are consistent and potentially clinically meaningful, the Cochrane reviewers concluded that these methodological s...
